-- title:
--   Independence of the zeroth determinantal ideal of a presentation
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $M$ an $R$-module. Suppose given two finite presentations of $M$ by free modules of finite rank: natural numbers $k,l$, a surjective $R$-linear map $\pi : R^{k} \to M$ (with $R^k$ realised as $\mathrm{Fin}\,k \to R$) and a matrix $X$ of size $k \times l$ over $R$ whose columns, i.e. the vectors $i \mapsto X_{ij}$ for $j$ ranging over $\mathrm{Fin}\,l$, span $\ker \pi$ as an $R$-submodule; and likewise natural numbers $k',l'$, a surjective $R$-linear map $\pi' : R^{k'} \to M$ and a $k' \times l'$ matrix $X'$ whose columns span $\ker \pi'$. The conclusion is an equality of two ideals of $R$: the ideal generated by all determinants $\det\bigl(X_{i\,c(j)}\bigr)_{i,j}$, as $c$ ranges over all functions $\mathrm{Fin}\,k \to \mathrm{Fin}\,l$ (so over all $k \times k$ submatrices of $X$ obtained by selecting $k$ columns, with repetitions allowed and order arbitrary), equals the ideal generated by all determinants $\det\bigl(X'_{i\,c(j)}\bigr)_{i,j}$ with $c : \mathrm{Fin}\,k' \to \mathrm{Fin}\,l'$. In particular, if $l < k$ every such determinant vanishes and both ideals are zero.
--
--   This is the invariance of the zeroth Fitting (determinantal) ideal $\mathrm{Fitt}_0(M)$, the ideal of maximal minors of a relation matrix, under change of finite presentation; it is stated concretely in terms of matrices, since Fitting ideals as such are not available. It is used, via the conormal presentation of the module of Kähler differentials, in [`PDivisibleGroup.associated_jacobianDet_pow_of_hasDimension_of_ringOfIntegers`](thm.html#PDivisibleGroup.associated_jacobianDet_pow_of_hasDimension_of_ringOfIntegers) to identify the Jacobian determinant of a square presentation up to units.
